1. Q: How can I wake up feeling less tired?
A: Drink a full glass of water within 5 minutes of waking up. Dehydration is a hidden cause of morning fatigue. Also step outside for 2 minutes of daylight — natural light resets your brain faster than coffee.

2. Q: What’s the fastest way to focus when I’m distracted?
A: Use the 5-minute rule: tell yourself you’ll work for just 5 minutes. Starting is the hardest part. Once you begin, your brain usually continues automatically.

3. Q: How can I remember names better?
A: Repeat the person’s name immediately in conversation:
“Nice to meet you, Sarah.”
Then connect it to an image or someone you already know with that name. Memory works through association.

4. Q: How do I stop procrastinating?
A: Break the task into a step so small it feels stupid not to do — open the document, write one sentence, sort one item. Momentum beats motivation.

5. Q: How can I fall asleep faster?
A: Try the 4-7-8 breathing method:
Inhale 4 seconds → hold 7 → exhale 8.
Repeat 4 times. It slows your nervous system and signals your body it’s safe to rest.

6. Q: What’s a simple way to save money without noticing?
A: Use the 24-hour rule for non-essential purchases. If you still want it tomorrow, buy it. Most impulse buys disappear overnight.

7. Q: How can I feel more confident instantly?
A: Adjust posture: shoulders back, chin level, slow breathing. Your body position feeds signals back to your brain. Confidence is partly physical.

8. Q: How do I make boring tasks more enjoyable?
A: Pair them with a reward: music, podcast, or favorite drink. Your brain starts linking the task with pleasure instead of resistance.

9. Q: What’s a quick way to reduce stress?
A: Name 5 things you see, 4 things you feel, 3 things you hear, 2 things you smell, 1 thing you taste. It pulls your mind out of anxiety and back into the present.

10. Q: How can I be more productive every day?
A: Pick the 1 task that matters most and do it first. Productivity isn’t doing more — it’s doing the right thing before distractions take over.

